<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Michael Fischer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1692–1766</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Michael Fischer was a Bavarian architect, one of the leading builders of the south German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> and Rococo. Based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, he designed or rebuilt dozens of churches and monasteries, using centralised plans and bright, unified interiors. Among his masterpieces are the abbey churches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottobeuren">Ottobeuren</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwiefalten">Zwiefalten</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> and Rott am Inn.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-michael-fischer.jpg" alt="Johann Michael Fischer" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burglengenfeld-standbild-johann-michael-fischer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
